139. Rxb2 b3 140. Rb1 Kb4 141. Kd2 Rc2+ 142. Kd3 Rg2 {Source: Glasgow Herald,
13 Oct 1928. In The Times, 5 Aug 1926, it was said to be more than 180 moves
long but this proves not to be the case. "We give below what we believe to be
the longest game ever played in the British Championship, though we have
recollections of another long one, in which Mr. V. Wahltuch, the one-time
Manchester expert, was a protagonist. Our game was played in 1926 in Edinburgh
between F. D. Yates and W. Gooding. The actual time taken, as proved by the
competitors' clocks, was 14 hours 11 minutes, and the moves numbered 142. This
is not a world's record, however, for a game was played at Carlsbad in 1907
which took 157 moves to complete. Whether more time was occupied we do not
know, probably there was, for as a rule play on the Continent is at a slower
rate than in Britain..."} 0-1

