Bg4 9. Be2 Bd7 10. a3 Nge7 11. Nd5 Nxd5 12. Qxd5 Be6 13. Qd1 Qc7 14. Bd3 Rd8
15. c4 Nd4 16. Be3 Nxf3+ 17. Qxf3 O-O 18. Rac1 a5 19. Qg3 Rc8 20. Rc2 Kh8 21.
Bxc5 Qxc5 22. Qf3 Bd7 23. Rc3 Qc7 24. Qe3 Rfe8 25. Kh1 b6 26. Qd2 Re6 27. f4
Rf6 28. f5 Bc6 29. g4 Qa7 30. Kg1 Rh6 31. g5 Rh5 32. Qg2 f6 33. gxf6 gxf6 34.
Rf3 Rg8 35. Rg3 Rhg5 36. Bc2 Qc7 37. b3 Qb7 38. Rce3 b5 39. Bd3 Qb6 40. Qf2 b4
41. a4 Qd4 42. Bc2 Rxg3+ 43. hxg3 Rg4 44. Qf3 Qa7 45. Kf2 Qg7 46. Qd1 Be8 47.
Qxd6 Qh6 48. Qd3 Qh2+ 49. Ke1 Qg1+ 50. Kd2 Rg7 51. Qe2 Rxg3 52. Rxg3 Qxg3 53.
Qe3 Qh2+ 54. Qe2 Qxe2+ 55. Kxe2 Kg8 56. Kf3 Kf8 57. Ke3 Ke7 58. Bd1 Kd6 59. Be2
Kc5 60. Kd3 h5 61. Bf3 h4 62. Bg4 Bf7 63. Ke3 Be8 {"The Ladies' Championship
attraction was the meeting between Miss Gilchrist and Miss Price. The game was
long and hard fought, both players were obviously aware of the probable
importance the result of the game would have on the destina­tion of the title,
and took no risks. Miss Price had, if anything, the pull in the mid-game, and
the ending was extremely interesting as it would appear that in a B and level
pawns ending there was an opportunity to force a win by sacrificing a passed 
[h-pawn]. Tired, and unable to see clearly to the finish of the combination,
Miss Price was content to draw." Source: The Scotsman, 4 August 1926} 1/2-1/2

